      "summary": "### Waste Valorization of Banana Peel and Used Cooking Oil for Biodegradable Bioplastic and Biopaper\n\n**Research Area:** Sustainable Biotechnology | Waste Valorization | Biodegradable Materials | Environmental Biotechnology\n\nThis research developed an integrated **waste-to-resource approach** for converting two abundant waste streams—**banana peels and used cooking oil—into biodegradable materials** as potential alternatives to conventional plastics.\n\nUsed cooking oil was first converted into biodiesel through transesterification, generating **crude glycerol as a valuable by-product**. The banana peel was then fractionated to recover **starch and fibrous residues**. The extracted starch was combined with crude glycerol to fabricate biodegradable **bioplastic films**, while the remaining fibrous fraction was utilized to develop **biopaper**.\n\nThe biodiesel was evaluated through **FTIR and turbidity analysis**, while the bioplastic was characterized using **FTIR, SEM, and TGA**, along with assessments of water absorption, pH resistance, and soil biodegradability. The results demonstrated the formation of a stable starch–glycerol matrix with promising functional properties and biodegradability.\n\nOverall, the study demonstrates a **circular waste-valorization strategy** that transforms locally available agro-industrial and food-service wastes into value-added biodegradable materials, highlighting its potential to reduce plastic pollution and support more sustainable material production in Bangladesh.\n\n"
